# The surface for box.obj. Model I/O reads this through the obj's `mtllib` line, which is
# how a bought asset carries its materials — the sample uses the same path rather than
# assigning textures in code, so that path is the one that gets tested.
# **`bump`, not `map_Bump`.** Model I/O's OBJ reader recognises only this
# spelling for a normal map: `map_Bump` — which is what Blender and Maya
# write — and `norm` are both read and silently dropped, with no property
# on the material and nothing logged.
newmtl crate
Kd 1 1 1
Ks 0 0 0
# planed timber: smoother than stone, far from polished. Ns 24 becomes roughness 0.53 — see Material.roughness(fromSpecularExponent:).
Ns 24
map_Kd crate.png
bump crate_normal.png
